/* Original practical teaching, supplementing the brief introductory chapter sections. */
const MORE_DEPTH={
5:[
['How a medical word is assembled','A prefix modifies the beginning, a root names a body part or process, and a suffix often tells what is happening. In bradycardia, brady means slow and cardia refers to the heart: a slow heart rate. Tachypnea means rapid breathing; tachy means fast and pnea relates to breathing. These word parts help decode unfamiliar language, but context still matters.','On a call, translate the term back into an observation: “breathing 32 times per minute” is clearer than “tachypneic” alone. Give numbers, time, and associated findings when possible.'],
['Common prefixes that change urgency','Brady means slow, tachy fast, hypo low, hyper high, and a or an without. Hypoglycemia is low blood glucose, while hyperglycemia is high blood glucose; the treatment implications can differ. Hypoxia means insufficient oxygen at tissue level. Apnea means absent breathing.','Read carefully before acting: confusing hypo with hyper reverses meaning. A label does not replace a glucose check, airway assessment, or overall clinical picture.'],
['Common suffixes in an EMS report','Itis indicates inflammation, emia a blood condition, pnea breathing, algia pain, and ectomy surgical removal. Dyspnea is difficult breathing; apnea is no breathing. A patient may report dyspnea even before an oximeter changes, so do not treat the suffix as a diagnosis.','Use a patient’s plain-language complaint alongside any medical term. If a patient says “I can’t catch my breath,” record it and describe rate, effort, and chest rise.'],
['Anatomic position and laterality','Right and left always mean the patient’s right and left while the body faces forward in standard anatomic position. Anterior means front and posterior back; medial means toward the midline and lateral away. Proximal is nearer the limb’s attachment, distal farther away.','“Swelling at the distal right forearm” is reproducible. Point to the spot while speaking with your partner to avoid a left-right error during handoff.'],
['Location words that matter during assessment','Superior and inferior describe headward and footward; superficial and deep describe distance below the surface. The chest is thoracic, the abdomen abdominal, and the pelvis contains lower urinary and reproductive structures. These are location words, not a substitute for examining the patient.','For a wound, report side, region, depth if safely visible, bleeding, tenderness, and distal circulation. Do not infer organ damage from the name of a region alone.'],
['Abdominal quadrants without premature diagnosis','Divide the abdomen into right and left upper and lower quadrants. The location of pain can suggest organs to consider, but illnesses can present atypically and pain can move or radiate. A patient’s report of where pain began can differ from where it is now.','Have the patient point, ask when the pain moved, and note guarding, distension, vomiting, and perfusion findings. Communicate findings without declaring a specific diagnosis.'],
['Positions and movement language','Supine means on the back, prone on the abdomen, lateral on the side, and upright with the torso raised. Flexion bends a joint; extension straightens it. A position can change breathing and comfort, and forcing a distressed patient flat may worsen symptoms.','Record the position found, the position used for care, and whether breathing or symptoms changed. Maintain motion precautions when indicated by mechanism and findings.'],
['Abbreviations and error prevention','Short forms can mean different things across services. BP, HR, RR, and SpO₂ are common, but any unfamiliar abbreviation should be checked. A precise verbal report beats a compact note that the receiving team cannot interpret.','Spell out an uncertain drug and dose, repeat critical numbers back, and state units. “Respiratory rate 8, shallow” communicates more than “low RR.”'],
['Turn a label into a measurable finding','“Stable,” “weak,” and “distress” are conclusions. Show the evidence behind them: alertness, rate and depth of breathing, pulse quality, skin, blood pressure, and trend. Symptoms are what the patient feels; signs are what you observe or measure.','Instead of “patient seems fine,” say “alert, speaking full sentences, breathing unlabored, skin warm, and pain unchanged.” Reassess; a single normal snapshot does not guarantee continued stability.'],
['Field example: translating a handoff','A partner says “hypotensive with tachycardia.” Ask for the actual values and trend: “BP 86/54, pulse 132 and weak, skin cool, down from BP 108/70 ten minutes ago.” The word parts flag possible poor perfusion, but the numbers and change guide priority.','In the chart, separate measured facts from interpretation. “Concern for shock” can follow the findings; it should not replace them.']
],
6:[
['The cell is the endpoint','Air entering the lungs is useful only if oxygen crosses the alveoli, attaches to hemoglobin, travels through circulation, and reaches cells. Cells use oxygen to make energy; poor delivery can arise from airway obstruction, lung disease, low blood volume, pump failure, or impaired blood flow.','Ask which link is failing when you see altered mental status or poor perfusion. Opening an airway, assisting ventilation, controlling bleeding, and rapid transport address different links in the same chain.'],
['Airway versus ventilation versus oxygenation','Airway patency means air can pass. Ventilation is movement of air in and out. Oxygenation concerns oxygen entering blood; respiration also includes gas exchange at tissues. A pulse oximeter estimates saturation, but it cannot by itself show whether enough air is moving or carbon dioxide is clearing.','A drowsy patient breathing slowly and shallowly may need BVM support despite a temporarily normal SpO₂. Look at chest rise, rate, effort, and mental status first.'],
['Upper and lower airway anatomy','Air passes through nose or mouth, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, and smaller airways before reaching alveoli. Tongue obstruction can produce snoring in an unresponsive patient; fluids can cause gurgling; upper airway narrowing may cause stridor. Lower airway narrowing can cause wheeze.','Match the sound to the likely problem without treating it as a diagnosis. Reposition and clear the airway, assess air movement, and escalate support when breathing is inadequate.'],
['How the chest draws in air','The diaphragm contracts downward and the rib cage expands, creating negative pressure. Chest injury, fatigue, obstruction, or reduced consciousness can limit this process. Visible effort is not the same as effective movement of air.','Watch symmetry and depth and listen for breath sounds. A patient working very hard with little chest rise is in trouble even if the respiratory rate appears high.'],
['The pump, pipes, and circulating volume','The right heart moves blood to lungs; the left heart sends oxygenated blood to the body. Arteries carry blood away and veins return it. Perfusion depends on an effective pump, enough circulating volume, and functioning vessels. Shock can occur when any part fails.','A rapid weak pulse with cool skin after bleeding points toward poor perfusion even while blood pressure is initially near normal. Reassess both circulation and the reason it may be failing.'],
['Blood components in practical terms','Red cells carry much of the oxygen through hemoglobin. Plasma supplies circulating fluid; platelets and clotting factors help stop bleeding. A normal SpO₂ percentage does not reveal how much blood remains or how well it reaches tissues.','After major hemorrhage, do not let a normal saturation reassure you. Control bleeding, examine skin and mental status, and expedite transport according to severity.'],
['Brain and spinal cord as control systems','The brainstem contributes to breathing drive and level of consciousness. The spinal cord carries movement and sensation signals. Trauma, low glucose, toxins, seizure, hypoxia, and poor perfusion can change responsiveness; a single cause cannot be assumed on scene.','Compare mental status with baseline, assess pupils and movement when indicated, and return to airway and breathing if the patient deteriorates.'],
['Autonomic responses and compensation','The body can constrict peripheral vessels and increase heart rate to defend blood flow to vital organs. That can produce cool clammy skin and tachycardia before a major blood pressure drop. Medicines such as beta blockers can blunt some expected signs.','Read the complete patient, not one number. Trend pulse, pressure, skin, mental status, and respiratory pattern together.'],
['Glucose and temperature regulation','Glucose supplies energy to the brain, so a low level can alter behavior or consciousness. Fever, cold exposure, and metabolic illness also change pulse and respiratory patterns. These systems overlap rather than acting in isolation.','Check glucose when indicated and authorized after immediate threats are addressed. Prevent heat loss in a vulnerable or shocked patient while avoiding a distracting search for one perfect diagnosis.'],
['Anatomy applied to a chest injury','A patient with a chest injury may have painful shallow breaths, asymmetric movement, and reduced air entry. The same injury may also affect circulation. The lung finding and the perfusion finding should be assessed together, not in separate silos.','Start with primary assessment, support breathing, look for bleeding and shock, repeat findings after movement, and communicate the trend in transport.']
],
7:[
['Infants: observe before touching','An infant cannot explain a complaint. Look at tone, interaction, cry, color, breathing effort, and feeding. A weak cry, poor tone, apnea, or poor perfusion is concerning. Normal heart and respiratory rates differ from adult values and change with age.','Ask the caregiver what the infant is usually like and whether intake, wet diapers, or activity changed. Treat an immediate airway or breathing problem before taking a full history.'],
['Why children can worsen quickly','Smaller airways are more affected by swelling and secretions. Children often increase breathing effort and heart rate to compensate; when they tire, movement of air can fall quickly. A quieter child after a prolonged struggle may be deteriorating.','Watch for retractions, nasal flaring, grunting, poor color, and reduced interaction. Recheck frequently and prepare age-appropriate ventilation equipment.'],
['The pediatric assessment triangle','A rapid first look considers appearance, work of breathing, and circulation to skin. Appearance includes tone, interactiveness, consolability, gaze, and cry. This is a first impression, not a replacement for a primary assessment or vital signs.','A child playing and interacting with normal work of breathing is different from one limp and staring. If any corner is abnormal, move promptly to ABCs and treatment.'],
['Talk to a preschool child and caregiver','Use short concrete explanations and simple choices when possible. Let a caregiver help calm the child without delaying care. A frightened child may resist equipment; observe breathing and color before upsetting them further if the condition permits.','Ask what is normal, when the change started, what the child may have swallowed or taken, and whether there was fever or injury. Verify medication and doses rather than assuming a household product is harmless.'],
['School age and adolescent assessment','School age patients can often report symptoms directly; invite them to point to pain and describe what changed. Adolescents may withhold details in front of family. Respect privacy and explain what you are doing while keeping safety and local consent rules in mind.','Address the patient, not only the adult in the room. For time-sensitive threats, act while another team member gathers history.'],
['Pregnancy changes the picture','Pregnancy changes circulation, breathing demands, and the meaning of abdominal pain or bleeding. Ask about gestational stage, complications, contractions, bleeding, and fetal movement when relevant. Do not ignore an injury or serious medical complaint because the patient appears young and healthy.','Prioritize maternal airway, breathing, and perfusion; transport and obstetric coordination follow local protocol. Avoid unsupported claims about fetal status from a brief field exam.'],
['Adults and baseline function','In a healthy adult, symptoms and vital signs can be compared with usual activity, but “normal for age” is not proof of safety. Medications, chronic illness, disability, and prior episodes alter presentation.','Ask what the patient could do yesterday versus today. New exercise intolerance, confusion, or shortness of breath matters even when the first vital set looks unremarkable.'],
['Older adults: less reserve, more confounders','Older adults may have multiple diseases and medicines, lower physiologic reserve, and atypical symptoms. Infection, dehydration, medication effects, and injury may first show as weakness, falls, or confusion. A normal age-related change should not be used to explain a new deficit.','Ask a caregiver for the usual mental status and mobility, review anticoagulants after a fall, and assess for occult injury and perfusion problems.'],
['Dementia and delirium are different questions','Dementia describes a chronic pattern; delirium is an acute change in attention or awareness. An older adult with dementia can also have a new treatable problem. The distinction relies on timeline and baseline from reliable witnesses, not a quick label.','If a nursing home says “always confused,” ask what the patient did earlier today. Note whether the current response to voice, speech, or walking differs from their baseline.'],
['Vital signs need age context','A child’s usual pulse and breathing rate vary by age, and fever or crying can change them. Hypotension can appear late in pediatric shock. Older adults taking rate-slowing medicines may not develop an expected tachycardia.','Use the age ranges and thresholds taught by your course and local service. Weight, size, appearance, and trend belong beside the numbers.']
],
8:[
['Decide whether the patient can be moved safely','A cooperative patient with no apparent injury may still be unable to walk because of dizziness, weakness, shock, or respiratory distress. Before movement, assess immediate threats and whether the move itself may worsen the condition.','Do not use “can you stand?” as a test on a patient who is faint, unstable, or severely injured. Bring the appropriate device and enough personnel.'],
['Choose an emergency move deliberately','An immediate hazard such as fire, traffic, structural danger, or inability to provide lifesaving care where the patient lies may require an emergency move. In a safe scene, take time to plan a nonurgent transfer and preserve alignment when indicated.','Name the hazard and destination aloud. Assign roles before moving. After the move, repeat primary assessment because airway, breathing, or bleeding may change.'],
['Plan the route before lifting','Check door widths, turns, stairs, surface, weather, lighting, and where the stretcher will be placed. Remove loose obstacles and secure oxygen, monitors, and lines. Confirm that the device is rated for the patient and terrain.','A short pause to clear a corner or request more hands prevents a stuck stretcher and unsafe improvisation.'],
['Use a leader and clear commands','The leader states the plan, checks that every member is ready, counts the lift, and can call a stop. Each member should say when grip, balance, or view is lost. Closed loop communication avoids one person moving while another is still setting up.','Before a stair carry, name who controls the head and foot, where the team will turn, and what to do if the patient changes.'],
['Mechanics that protect the crew','Keep feet apart, load close, and bend at hips and knees while keeping the trunk controlled. Avoid twisting under load; pivot the feet. Use mechanical aids whenever available. Do not assume a patient’s weight from appearance.','If a lift is beyond the team, stop and call for help. Crew injury can create a second patient and delay care.'],
['Stretcher and stair chair checkpoints','Lock wheels before transfer, lower and secure the stretcher per manufacturer instructions, and use straps correctly. Stair chairs are useful in confined stairs for appropriate patients, but the device choice depends on condition and local training.','Recheck airway, breathing, perfusion, and comfort after securing. Avoid leaving a patient unattended on a raised or unlocked device.'],
['Suspected spine injury and motion restriction','Use the mechanism and findings to decide whether spinal motion precautions are indicated under local protocol. Minimize unnecessary movement while maintaining airway and correcting life threats. Rigid immobilization can cause harm if applied indiscriminately.','An unsafe scene or failing airway takes precedence over a perfect motion-restriction setup. Communicate the reason for the selected technique.'],
['Patient dignity and communication','Explain what you will do, ask about pain and mobility, maintain warmth and privacy, and warn before each movement when time permits. Patients may be frightened by being lifted or strapped.','Have a teammate watch the patient rather than everyone watching the device. Document new pain, dizziness, or breathing change during transfer.'],
['When a lift goes wrong','If a member loses grip or the patient slips, call a stop and stabilize the load if safe. Do not try to “push through” a failing maneuver. Reassess for injury and plan a safer second attempt with help or equipment.','A controlled reset is safer than a rushed recovery. Report any new patient complaint and check equipment before the next move.'],
['Field example: a narrow staircase','An older patient is weak, short of breath, and two flights up. Start with primary assessment and needed treatment. Check whether the patient can sit, whether oxygen or ventilation can continue during movement, and whether a chair and extra crew are needed.','Agree on route and roles, secure the device, move in stages, and reassess after each major change of position.']
],
9:[
['Team roles at the start of a call','Give one person overall coordination while assigning airway, assessment, treatment, equipment, documentation, and family communication as resources allow. Roles can change when the situation changes. An explicit role prevents duplicated work and missed tasks.','Say “I am managing the airway; you check bleeding and pulse; please bring the BVM.” Everyone should still report any threat they see.'],
['Closed loop communication in action','The leader gives a clear task to a named person, that person repeats it, then confirms completion and result. This is especially useful when the scene is noisy or many people are present.','“Ari, prepare suction.” “Preparing suction.” “Suction ready.” If a task is not completed, say so promptly rather than letting silence imply success.'],
['Share a common mental picture','A team needs the same patient priority, immediate threat, and next step. Briefly state the working concern and evidence, then invite correction. A partner may notice a new finding while the lead clinician focuses on one task.','“The patient has a pulse but shallow breathing; we are assisting ventilation and moving to transport.” Revisit that statement if the patient worsens.'],
['Speak up without a hierarchy barrier','A junior member who sees inadequate chest rise, renewed bleeding, or a medication mismatch should state it directly. The leader should acknowledge and recheck. Respectful challenge prevents fixation and errors.','Use observation plus request: “I do not see chest rise with the bag. Can we reposition and check the seal?”'],
['Prioritize tasks in parallel','One responder can manage the immediate threat while another obtains a witness history, monitors vitals, or prepares transport. Parallel work only helps if the primary intervention remains effective and communication is explicit.','Do not leave a struggling airway unattended to complete paperwork or a medication list. Assign the history after lifesaving actions begin.'],
['The handoff is a transfer of responsibility','Present who the patient is, what happened and when, the initial and current assessment, key history, treatment, and response. Flag uncertainties and changes. The receiver should be able to repeat the immediate concern.','Instead of “stable now,” say “RR rose from 8 shallow to 16 with assisted breaths; pulse remains present; last medication time unknown.”'],
['Medication safety is a team task','Before an authorized medication, confirm the drug, concentration, patient, indication, dose, route, time, and contraindications. Speak aloud when a label or history is uncertain. Record who gave it and the response.','A teammate can independently verify an important dose or allergy when workflow permits. Never let urgency turn an illegible label into a guess.'],
['Work with ALS and receiving teams','A basic crew can continue airway support, compressions, bleeding control, movement, vitals, and clear communication while higher-level clinicians perform their procedures. Ask what role is needed rather than abandoning your task.','When an ALS team arrives, summarize threats and treatments, then continue assigned support. Avoid repeating a long story while ventilation or CPR quality falls.'],
['Debrief to improve the next call','After the patient is transferred, a short no-blame review can identify what went well, a near miss, and one change for next time. Separate facts from hindsight and capture equipment or communication problems.','“We found the airway problem early, but the suction was not ready; next time we assign that at the door.”'],
['Field example: two patients, one crew','At a crash, one patient has severe bleeding and the other is shouting. Communicate patient count and resource needs, use the local triage framework, assign one person to bleeding control, and avoid losing situational awareness.','State who is caring for whom and when reassessment occurs. The loudest patient is not automatically the sickest.']
],
11:[
['A quick airway problem map','Snoring often points to soft tissue in the upper airway; gurgling to fluid; stridor to upper airway narrowing; wheeze to lower airway narrowing. These sounds are clues, not diagnoses. Silence with poor chest rise can be especially dangerous because little air is moving.','Open and clear the airway, assess rate and depth, and decide whether the patient needs oxygen, assisted ventilation, or both. Reassess after every intervention.'],
['Positioning and airway opening','A head tilt chin lift can open an airway when spinal injury is not suspected. A jaw thrust may limit neck movement when trauma is a concern, but oxygenation and ventilation still take priority if it fails. Position a conscious distressed patient for the easiest breathing when safe.','Watch chest rise after each maneuver. If there is no improvement, look for obstruction, secretions, mask-seal trouble, or a need for additional help.'],
['Suction: what problem it solves','Blood, vomit, or secretions can block airflow and interfere with a mask seal. Choose equipment sized to the patient and follow your training for insertion and timing. Suction only as needed, then ventilate and reassess; prolonged suction can interrupt oxygen delivery.','Gurgling that persists after one attempt means the airway needs another look. Prepare suction before a high-risk patient vomits, rather than waiting until the view is lost.'],
['OPA and NPA: match device to patient','An OPA is used in an unresponsive patient without a gag reflex; size and placement matter. An NPA may be tolerated when some gag reflex remains, but facial injury or suspected skull-base injury may make it inappropriate under your protocol. Neither device fixes fluid in the airway.','If insertion causes gagging, bleeding, or worsening obstruction, stop and reassess. The adjunct is a tool to keep a passage open, not a substitute for ventilation.'],
['Oxygen devices in plain language','A nasal cannula and a nonrebreather rely on the patient’s own breaths. A nonrebreather supplies higher oxygen concentration when used and flowing correctly, but it does not create breaths. A BVM pushes air into the lungs when spontaneous breathing is absent or inadequate.','Choose support from actual breathing rate, depth, effort, chest rise, mental status, and oxygenation rather than a single SpO₂ threshold. Follow local oxygen targets and device setup.'],
['A one-minute breathing decision','Ask: Is the airway open? Is the patient moving enough air? Are there signs of low oxygen? If ventilation is inadequate, assist it. If ventilation is adequate but oxygenation is impaired, oxygen may help. Recheck the answer when the patient tires or treatment starts.','A patient breathing 6 times per minute with weak rise needs assisted ventilation even if a previously measured saturation is normal.'],
['BVM technique that actually moves air','Pick a mask that covers nose and mouth without covering the eyes, open the airway, form a seal, and deliver only enough volume to see chest rise. Two-person BVM lets one responder maintain a two-hand seal while another squeezes the bag. Add oxygen and reservoir according to equipment and protocol.','If the chest does not rise, fix position, seal, or obstruction rather than simply squeezing harder. Excessive force or speed increases risk of stomach inflation.'],
['Rates and reassessment during assisted ventilation','Follow current age-specific BLS and local instruction for timing. For an adult with a definite pulse but no normal breathing, AHA adult BLS uses one breath about every six seconds; check pulse about every two minutes. The goal is visible chest rise without hyperventilation.','If the pulse is lost or not definite in the prescribed brief check, transition to cardiac arrest care. Check ventilation effectiveness continuously, not only when the timer sounds.'],
['Pulse oximetry has blind spots','The reading estimates oxygen saturation in circulating hemoglobin. Motion, poor perfusion, cold fingers, nail products, and device delay can mislead. It does not directly measure tidal volume, carbon dioxide, or total oxygen delivery.','Compare the tracing and reading with the patient. A slow, shallow, drowsy patient should not wait for the number to fall before receiving ventilation support.'],
['Airway field example','An adult with a pulse is drowsy, snoring, and breathing shallowly. Open the airway, clear secretions if present, assess rise and rate, and support ventilation if inadequate. A partner can gather event and medication history while you protect the airway.','After each change, state whether chest rise, rate, and responsiveness improved. If not, revisit positioning, adjunct choice, seal, and transport urgency.']
],
12:[
['The first medication question is authorization','An EMT’s permitted medicines and routes vary by jurisdiction, service, medical direction, and training. A medication familiar from another country or online guide may be outside your local scope. The chapter teaches safe reasoning, not an independent prescription list.','Before touching a drug, verify the local protocol or order, patient, indication, available formulation, and your role. If uncertain, ask medical direction.'],
['The six rights as a safety pause','Confirm the right patient, medication, dose, route, time, and documentation. Also check the indication, allergies, contraindications, prior doses, and whether the medicine is expired or has the wrong concentration. A correct drug at the wrong strength can still harm a patient.','Read the container label and verbalize the dose and route. For a critical uncertainty, stop and verify instead of guessing from package color.'],
['Indication, contraindication, and precaution','An indication is a reason a medicine might help; a contraindication is a reason to avoid it. A precaution requires extra consideration or direction. These depend on the patient, presentation, other medicines, and route—not merely the name of the complaint.','The patient says “I always take this for chest pain” but is now hypotensive: check the specific medication’s contraindications under your protocol before assisting.'],
['Allergies: ask what happened','“I am allergic” is useful but incomplete. Ask which drug or substance and what reaction occurred: mild nausea differs from swelling, wheeze, or a prior severe reaction. Do not use a vague answer to dismiss risk.','Pass the substance and reaction to the team. If the patient cannot speak, use reliable records or a witness while immediate threats are treated.'],
['Dose, concentration, and units','A dose is the amount delivered; concentration is how much drug is in a given volume or device. Check units carefully and do not swap milligrams, milliliters, micrograms, or device counts. Weight-based calculations require an accurate weight and training.','Repeat a calculated or unusual dose aloud when a partner is available. Document exactly what was given and by which route, not just the product name.'],
['Routes change speed and safety','Oral medicine requires the ability to swallow and protect the airway. Inhaled medication must reach the lungs; intranasal absorption and auto-injector use have device-specific steps. Each route has different onset, limitations, and risk.','A confused patient who cannot follow swallowing commands should not receive oral glucose. Follow the authorized alternative and keep airway care first.'],
['Therapeutic effect versus adverse effect','A therapeutic effect is the intended benefit; a side effect is an expected but unwanted action; an adverse reaction may be dangerous. Look for both improvement in the original problem and new airway, breathing, circulation, or mental status problems.','After any medication, note time, vital signs, symptoms, and response. A new wheeze, rash, or deterioration triggers immediate reassessment.'],
['Helping with a patient’s own medication','Assisting an existing prescribed medicine is still a clinical act with a protocol, correct patient and dose, and contraindications. The patient’s request alone does not authorize it. Verify what was already taken and whether the device is theirs.','A patient asks for another inhaler dose: confirm the substance, recent doses, indication, breathing status, and local direction.'],
['Naloxone does not replace ventilation','For suspected opioid toxicity with inadequate breathing and a pulse, opening the airway and assisting ventilation address the immediate problem. Naloxone, when authorized, may help reverse opioid effects but can take time and the patient can deteriorate again.','Continue monitoring breathing, pulse, and mental status after dosing. Do not wait for a drug to work while breaths remain ineffective.'],
['Medication field example and handoff','An adult with severe wheeze requests an inhaler. Check airway and ventilation first, then assess the medication and authorization, allergies, prior use, and contraindications. Assist only within protocol, then compare respiratory effort, speech, air movement, pulse, and symptoms.','In handoff: name, dose, route, time, why it was given, and the response. Report a lack of improvement rather than assuming success.']
],
13:[
['Perfusion is the real target','Shock means tissue blood flow and oxygen delivery are not meeting demand. Blood pressure is a useful signal, not the whole definition. The body may defend pressure for a time while hands get cool, pulse speeds up, and mental status changes.','Look for the cluster and trend. A patient with severe bleeding and normal first BP may still be developing shock.'],
['Four broad mechanisms','Hypovolemic shock involves too little circulating volume, often hemorrhage or fluid loss. Cardiogenic shock involves pump failure. Distributive shock involves abnormal vessel dilation, including severe infection or anaphylaxis. Obstructive shock involves blocked filling or outflow. An EMT need not prove the subtype before addressing threats.','Ask what happened and look for bleeding, chest symptoms, infection, allergic exposure, or chest injury. Do not let classification delay transport.'],
['Compensated versus decompensated','Early compensation can produce tachycardia, increased breathing, pale cool skin, and anxiety while pressure is preserved. Later signs can include falling pressure, weak pulses, worsening confusion, and reduced responsiveness. Individual patterns differ with age and medicines.','A rising heart rate and cooling skin across ten minutes matter even if the BP has not crossed a memorized threshold.'],
['Find external bleeding fast','Expose enough to look for major hemorrhage, including beneath clothing and behind the patient when safe. Apply firm direct pressure. For life-threatening extremity bleeding, use a tourniquet as trained; wound packing with sustained pressure may be appropriate for some junctional wounds under training.','Recheck that bleeding actually stopped and record a tourniquet time. Do not remove an effective device casually; follow local protocol.'],
['Internal bleeding may be invisible','A fall, crash, abdominal injury, pelvic injury, or gastrointestinal bleeding may cause volume loss without blood on the floor. Pain, distension, bruising, weak pulse, cool skin, or a falling level of consciousness can be clues, but none alone proves the source.','Treat the perfusion problem and transport priority. Do not delay for a long on-scene search when the patient is deteriorating.'],
['Anaphylaxis can be shock and airway trouble','Exposure followed by breathing difficulty, swelling, rash, vomiting, or poor perfusion can indicate a severe allergic reaction. A rash is not required, and a hoarse voice or swelling can signal a rapidly changing airway.','Assess airway and breathing, request help, and follow the locally authorized epinephrine pathway promptly. Reassess perfusion and breathing after treatment.'],
['Sepsis may have a different skin picture','Severe infection can cause distributive shock. Fever may be present, absent, or less obvious in older or immunocompromised patients. Altered mental status, weakness, abnormal breathing, and perfusion changes deserve attention.','Ask about recent illness and baseline while checking primary threats and arranging timely transport. Do not wait for a thermometer to prove the cause.'],
['Support without overpromising a cure','Control bleeding, support airway and ventilation, avoid heat loss, position according to condition, and expedite definitive care. Oxygen use should be guided by clinical need and protocol. Some shock causes need interventions beyond EMT scope.','Reassess pulse, skin, mental status, breathing, pressure, and bleeding. Tell the receiving team what is worsening and when.'],
['Why oral intake is a bad shortcut','A shocked or deteriorating patient may vomit, aspirate, or need urgent procedures. Giving food or water does not reliably correct acute perfusion failure and can complicate care.','Follow local protocol rather than offering a drink to a patient who is pale, confused, and becoming weaker.'],
['Shock field example','A patient after a crash is alert with BP 112/72, pulse 112, cool hands, and thigh pain. Ten minutes later pulse is 134 and the patient is confused. The trajectory is concerning even before a second BP is obtained.','Repeat primary assessment, look for bleeding, treat accessible threats, prevent heat loss, and expedite transport with a trend-based handoff.']
],
14:[
['The arrest decision in seconds','Check responsiveness and normal breathing. Occasional gasps are not normal breathing. A trained clinician checks for a pulse for no more than ten seconds; if there is no definite pulse, begin compressions and send for the AED.','A prolonged pulse search costs time. Assign one person to compressions while another brings equipment and calls for help.'],
['Compressions: rate, depth, recoil','For adults, AHA BLS recommends 100–120 compressions per minute, at least 5 cm or 2 inches deep while avoiding excessive depth, full chest recoil, and minimal interruptions. Hands stay on the lower half of the sternum.','Rotate compressors when fatigued if the team can do so with a brief pause. Poor depth or leaning reduces blood flow even if the count sounds right.'],
['Breaths during CPR','Use the current ratio and age-specific guidance taught by your course. Open the airway and give breaths that produce visible chest rise, avoiding rapid or excessive ventilation. With an advanced airway, the sequence differs; follow trained team roles.','If the chest does not rise, fix airway position and seal promptly while minimizing interruption to compressions.'],
['AED: prepare, analyze, clear, resume','Turn the AED on, expose and dry the chest as needed, apply pads as pictured, and follow prompts. During analysis and any shock, nobody touches the patient. Resume CPR when directed, whether or not a shock was advised.','A “no shock” message does not mean the patient has a pulse. Reassess only when the algorithm directs and minimize pauses.'],
['Pulse present, breathing absent','A definite pulse with absent or inadequate breathing calls for ventilation, not compressions solely for that reason. For an adult, AHA BLS uses one breath about every six seconds and pulse checks about every two minutes. Provide enough volume for visible chest rise.','If the pulse becomes absent or uncertain in the brief reassessment, transition to cardiac arrest care.'],
['Opioid suspicion during resuscitation','A suspected opioid exposure does not change the need for airway support, ventilation, CPR when pulseless, and prompt AED use. Naloxone may be given under protocol without interrupting these priorities.','A dose is not a substitute for effective breaths. Keep checking chest rise and pulse and watch for recurrent respiratory depression.'],
['Mild versus severe choking','An effective cough and ability to speak suggest a partial obstruction; encourage coughing and watch closely. Inability to speak, weak or absent cough, and poor airflow indicate severe obstruction. Activate emergency help and intervene using current guidance.','Do not do a blind finger sweep. A patient who was coughing effectively can worsen suddenly, so reassess continuously.'],
['Adult severe foreign-body obstruction','The 2025 AHA adult guidance uses repeated cycles of five back blows followed by five abdominal thrusts until relief or unresponsiveness. When the abdomen cannot be encircled or in late pregnancy, use five back blows followed by five chest thrusts.','When the person becomes unresponsive, begin CPR starting with compressions and remove an object only if visible when opening the airway. Pediatric techniques differ.'],
['After return of spontaneous circulation','A patient with a pulse after an arrest still needs airway, breathing, perfusion, mental status, and repeated assessment. Support ventilation if inadequate and coordinate with advanced care and transport.','Report the arrest timeline, CPR and AED events, shocks, medicines, and the change in status without treating a regained pulse as the end of the call.'],
['Arrest field example','A patient collapses at a meal, is unresponsive, gasping, and has no definite pulse in ten seconds. Start compressions and bring the AED. A teammate manages airway and breaths according to the team’s training.','After each analysis or shock, resume compressions as prompted. If a definite pulse returns, reassess breathing and support it as needed.']
],
15:[
['Start broad, then narrow','First manage airway, breathing, perfusion, bleeding, and mental status. Then use the chief complaint, onset, associated symptoms, medication history, exam, and vital trends to build a short list of possible processes. The field goal is safe prioritization, not proving a diagnosis.','A patient labeled “anxiety” can still have hypoxia, a cardiac problem, low glucose, or another urgent cause. Check findings before accepting the label.'],
['Altered mental status: a practical search','Consider inadequate oxygenation or ventilation, poor perfusion, glucose disturbance, stroke, seizure, poisoning, infection, and trauma. Ask witnesses about baseline and when it changed. A glucose check may be useful when authorized, but it does not replace primary care.','An unresponsive patient with shallow breathing gets airway and ventilation support before a detailed differential.'],
['Seizure and the postictal period','During a seizure, protect from injury and time the event; do not restrain forcefully or put anything in the mouth. Afterward, reassess airway, breathing, trauma, glucose when indicated, and recovery of mental status. Persistent or repeated seizure activity is high priority.','Ask witnesses about duration, prior seizures, medications, injury, and whether the patient returned to baseline.'],
['Chest discomfort and atypical symptoms','Pressure, pain, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, and weakness can all be relevant; some patients may not describe classic central chest pain. Ask OPQRST, assess breathing and perfusion, and follow your local chest-pain pathway.','Do not declare “heartburn” from location or relief after one treatment. Report onset, associated findings, vitals, and change.'],
['Stroke-like symptoms and timing','New face, arm, speech, balance, or vision changes warrant a focused neurologic screen and the local stroke pathway. Establish last known well rather than merely discovery time. Glucose can mimic stroke and may be checked if authorized without delaying action.','A negative brief screen does not exclude all stroke. Avoid delaying transport for an exhaustive exam.'],
['Glucose problems and oral safety','Low glucose can cause sweating, confusion, weakness, seizure, or unconsciousness. High glucose and other illness can also cause altered status. Measure when indicated and authorized, then use the correct local treatment route.','Do not give oral glucose to someone unable to swallow or protect the airway. Continue reassessment after treatment because symptoms may persist or recur.'],
['Allergic reaction versus anaphylaxis','A mild isolated rash differs from a reaction involving airway, breathing, circulation, or multiple systems. Hoarseness, wheeze, swelling, vomiting, and poor perfusion after exposure raise concern for a severe reaction even without dramatic hives.','Treat immediate threats, use authorized epinephrine and other care per protocol, and transport with repeated airway and perfusion checks.'],
['Poisoning and overdose','Scene safety matters with fumes, chemicals, or unknown substances. Obtain what was taken, amount, time, route, and packaging without delaying airway and breathing support. More than one substance may be involved.','Do not assume naloxone will fix every altered patient. Support ventilation when inadequate and bring the container or photograph of label when safe and appropriate.'],
['Fever and infection in a sick patient','Fever, cough, urinary symptoms, wounds, or recent illness can help explain a presentation, but sepsis may first show as confusion, weakness, abnormal breathing, or poor perfusion. Older adults may not have a dramatic fever.','Use appropriate PPE, assess ABCs, trend vitals, and communicate concern and timing.'],
['Medical field example: dizziness with a new deficit','An older adult reports familiar dizziness, but a partner notices new slurred speech and arm drift. The new objective findings change the priority. Establish last known well, assess ABCs and glucose as indicated, and follow the stroke pathway.','Report what is new versus baseline. A prior diagnosis of vertigo must not anchor this encounter.']
],
16:[
['Distress and failure are different','Distress is increased work while the patient can still sustain ventilation. Failure means air movement is inadequate or the effort is collapsing. Worsening fatigue, shallow breaths, poor chest rise, altered mental status, and a quieter chest are more concerning than a high respiratory rate alone.','A patient who stops struggling but becomes drowsy may be deteriorating. Reassess immediately and prepare assisted ventilation.'],
['Observe before the mask goes on','Count breaths and judge depth, rhythm, accessory muscle use, retractions, position, color, ability to speak, and level of consciousness. Listen to breath sounds and compare sides. These observations establish the baseline against which treatment is judged.','“RR 30 with one-word speech and poor air entry” is more useful than “short of breath.”'],
['Wheeze, crackles, stridor, and silence','Wheeze often reflects narrowed lower airways; crackles may reflect fluid or other lung processes; stridor suggests upper airway narrowing. Diminished or absent sounds can signal very poor movement or a localized problem. These are clues interpreted with the whole patient.','Do not diagnose asthma from wheeze alone or call a quieter chest improvement without checking air movement and status.'],
['Asthma and obstructive patterns','Ask about prior asthma or COPD, usual inhalers, triggers, prior severe episodes, and what was taken today. Work of breathing, speech, air movement, and fatigue guide severity. Authorized bronchodilator care follows local indication and contraindication checks.','If the patient becomes drowsy or barely moves air, support ventilation and expedite; do not wait for repeated inhaler attempts to succeed.'],
['Fluid and cardiac clues','Breathlessness with crackles, swelling, or a history of heart failure may suggest fluid-related problems, but there may be other causes. Assess blood pressure, perfusion, breathing, position, and relevant history without declaring a definitive diagnosis.','Use locally authorized care and transport. A patient who cannot lie flat may breathe better upright if safe; monitor for falling pressure or fatigue.'],
['Infection and environmental exposure','Fever, cough, sick contacts, or recent aspiration can point toward infection. Smoke, carbon monoxide, fumes, and other inhaled exposures can make the scene dangerous and may not produce a reliable pulse oximeter picture.','Protect responders, move to safety when indicated, support airway and breathing, and report the exposure to receiving care.'],
['Oxygen is not a ventilator','Supplemental oxygen helps when the patient is breathing adequately but needs more oxygen. A nasal cannula or nonrebreather cannot compensate for absent or very shallow breathing. A BVM assists ventilation and is used with oxygen according to protocol.','Check chest rise, rate, effort, mental status, and SpO₂ trend together. A temporarily normal reading does not justify leaving inadequate breaths untreated.'],
['BVM support and failure points','Position the airway, size and seal the mask, and give breaths for visible chest rise at the trained rate. If ventilation is ineffective, consider secretions, obstruction, positioning, mask leak, or device problems. A two-person seal may help.','Recheck after each correction. Forceful fast bagging can inflate the stomach and reduce effective care.'],
['Pulse oximetry and its limitations','SpO₂ is an estimate and may lag behind a sudden change. Motion and poor perfusion can distort a reading; it does not measure carbon dioxide clearance. A patient can be hypercapnic and tiring even while receiving oxygen.','Treat the patient’s ventilation and mental status, not only the screen. Repeat assessment after every treatment or position change.'],
['Respiratory field example','An adult sits upright, wheezes loudly, and speaks in phrases. After treatment, wheezing is faint, chest rise is poor, and the patient speaks only one word and becomes sleepy. This is a warning pattern, not reliable improvement.','Reassess airway and ventilation, support breaths if inadequate, call for help according to local system, and communicate the trend early.']
]
};
